## Updated strategy (simple rule‑based policy)

The new rule set keeps the successful parts of the current policy, adds a **“taper‑near‑goal”** clause, and makes the left‑side push a little more aggressive (‑1.0) to guarantee enough kinetic energy.

```
IF    p >= 0.45                     THEN a = 0.0                         # goal reached
ELIF  p >= 0.40                     THEN a = 0.3                         # gentle push, just enough to cross
ELIF  v > 0   AND p < 0.40          THEN a = 0.6                         # steady right thrust
ELIF  v <= 0  AND p > -0.20         THEN a = -0.8                        # brake when we start sliding back
ELIF  p <= -0.20                    THEN a = -1.0                        # maximal left push to gain speed
ELSE                                 a = 0.6                         # default right thrust
```

### Why this works

| Condition | Rationale |
|-----------|-----------|
| `p >= 0.45` | Stop immediately – no more penalty. |
| `p >= 0.40` | We are only a few centimeters from the goal; a small thrust (`0.3`) is enough to cross, and the penalty `‑0.1·0.3² ≈ ‑0.009` is negligible compared with the +100 reward. |
| `v>0` & `p<0.40` | While moving right and still far from the summit we keep the proven “sweet‑spot” thrust `0.6`. It is strong enough to keep the car climbing but cheap in terms of penalty (`‑0.036`). |
| `v≤0` & `p>‑0.20` | If the velocity has become negative on the right side we apply a modest brake (`‑0.8`). This prevents the car from sliding back too far while still keeping the penalty low. |
| `p ≤ ‑0.20` | Deep in the left valley we need the maximum leftward force to build the kinetic energy required for the next swing. |
| `else` | Catch‑all: any other state (e.g., slightly negative velocity but still left of `‑0.20`) we continue with the right‑ward `0.6`. |

