| **Previous** | **93.77** | The car is able to climb the right hill and terminate with the +100 bonus. The rule set is simple: once the car is moving forward (v > 0) it always applies a moderate forward thrust (a = 0.6) until the position reaches ≈0.40, where the thrust is reduced to 0.3. When the car is moving backward on the left side it applies a strong reverse push (a = ‑1.0) to build momentum. This “push‑forward‑until‑you‑reach‑the‑top” pattern works, but the constant 0.6 thrust is a little aggressive – it adds unnecessary ‑0.06 penalty per step. |
| **Best (so far)** | **93.97** | Same overall shape as the previous one, but with a finer‑grained “mid‑hill” region (p ≥ 0.35 → a = 0.5). The extra reduction in thrust when the car is already close to the goal lowers the cumulative action‑penalty and therefore yields a slightly higher net reward. The rule set also contains a few redundant clauses (e.g., *v > 0 AND p ≤ ‑0.20 → a = 0.6*), which do not hurt performance but make the policy harder to read. |

**What worked?**  

* **Positive‑velocity forward thrust** – as soon as the car has a forward velocity it should keep pushing forward, but the magnitude should be reduced once the car is already close to the goal (p ≈ 0.35‑0.45).  
* **Strong reverse thrust only when the car is still stuck on the left side** – a full‑reverse push (‑1.0) is needed to gain enough kinetic energy to climb out of the left valley, but it should be applied **only while the velocity is still non‑positive**; once the car has started moving forward, a reverse push only wastes reward.  
* **Gradual tapering of the forward force** – the switch from 0.6 → 0.5 → 0.3 as the car approaches the top reduces the ‑0.1·a² penalty without sacrificing the ability to reach the goal.

**What did not work?**  

* Applying a reverse force *even when the velocity is already positive* (the “‑1.0” clause in the current strategy) drives the car back toward the left wall and kills momentum.  
* Keeping the forward thrust at the maximum value (0.6) all the way to the top adds extra penalty; the car can reach the goal with a slightly smaller force once it is already high on the hill.  
* Several overlapping conditions create “dead zones” where the policy falls back to a = 0.0 (e.g., the first rule *IF p ≥ 0.45 THEN a = 0.0* is fine, but the later *ELSE a = 0.0* can be reached when the car is already moving forward but still far from the hill, causing the car to stall.)

---

## Updated, compact policy (the one we will use from now on)

The new rule list keeps the successful ideas, removes the contradictory clauses and adds a small “coasting” region (a = 0.0) when the car is already moving fast enough forward (v ≥ 0.02) and is past the mid‑hill point. This further reduces the action‑penalty while still guaranteeing that the car will not lose momentum.

```
IF    p >= 0.45                         THEN a = 0.0                # goal reached
ELIF  p >= 0.40  AND v > 0.00           THEN a = 0.3                # near the top, gentle push
ELIF  p >= 0.35  AND v > 0.00           THEN a = 0.5                # upper hill, moderate push
ELIF  p >= 0.20  AND v > 0.00           THEN a = 0.6                # middle of the hill, strong push
ELIF  v > 0.02  AND p >= 0.20           THEN a = 0.0                # coasting – enough speed, save reward
ELIF  v <= 0.00  AND p <= -0.20         THEN a = -1.0               # still stuck on left, full reverse
ELIF  v <= 0.00  AND p >  -0.20         THEN a = -0.8               # sliding back toward left wall, strong reverse
ELIF  v > 0.00  AND p <  -0.20          THEN a = 0.6                # just left the valley, boost forward
ELSE                                      a = 0.0                # safety net
```

### Why this should be better

| Condition | Intended effect |
|-----------|-----------------|
| **p ≥ 0.45** | Terminates the episode without any extra force. |
| **0.40 ≤ p < 0.45 & v > 0** | Reduce thrust to 0.3 – just enough to cross the finish line, minimal penalty. |
| **0.35 ≤ p < 0.40 & v > 0** | 0.5 is sufficient to keep climbing while shaving off a little extra cost. |
| **0.20 ≤ p < 0.35 & v > 0** | Full forward thrust (0.6) to build kinetic energy. |
| **v > 0.02 & p ≥ 0.20** | When the car already has a decent forward speed (> 0.02 m/s) we let it coast (a = 0) to avoid the ‑0.1·a² penalty. |
| **v ≤ 0 & p ≤ ‑0.20** | Still stuck at the bottom – give the maximum reverse push to start the “rock‑back‑and‑forth” swing. |
| **v ≤ 0 & -0.20 < p** | The car is sliding back toward the left wall; a strong (‑0.8) reverse push accelerates the next forward swing. |
| **v > 0 & p < ‑0.20** | The car just escaped the left valley; keep a solid forward thrust (0.6) to convert the swing into upward motion. |
| **else** | Default to zero force – prevents accidental large actions when none of the above apply. |

---

### What to monitor next

* **Average episode length** – with the coasting clause we expect slightly longer episodes (more steps) but a higher net reward because the action‑penalty is reduced.  
* **Distribution of velocities around p ≈ 0.30‑0.40** – if the car frequently stalls before reaching 0.45, we can raise the coasting threshold (e.g., require v ≥ 0.03) or increase the forward thrust in the 0.35‑0.40 band to 0.55.  
* **Frequency of the “‑0.8” reverse action** – if it appears too often, the car is spending too much time sliding back; we could tighten the left‑side condition to p > ‑0.15 before switching to –0.8.

With these observations in mind, the above policy should consistently reproduce the ~94‑95 net reward that we saw with the “best” strategy while shaving off a few extra points of penalty, and it is far easier to reason about and extend if needed.
