**Reflection on the three rule‑based policies**

| Policy | Avg. reward (10 trials) | Typical behaviour |
|--------|------------------------|-------------------|
| **Policy 1** (the one you are currently using) | **92.87** | Strong positive thrust ( 0.6 ) as soon as the car has any forward velocity, even when it is still far left of the valley. The “‑0.8” reverse thrust is applied whenever the velocity is negative, regardless of how far the car is from the left wall. |
| **Policy 2** (previous version) | **93.95** | Adds a few extra “‑1.0” pushes when the car is deep in the left side ( p ≤ ‑0.20 & v ≤ 0 ). The extra thrust does not improve the score – the larger penalty (‑0.1 a²) outweighs the tiny gain in speed. |
| **Policy 3** (best so far) | **93.97** | Keeps the reverse thrust at **‑0.8** (never uses the harsher ‑1.0), and tapers the forward thrust as the car approaches the goal (0.6 → 0.5 → 0.3). This policy reaches the top with the smallest possible cumulative penalty, which is why it scores the highest. |

**What worked?**

1. **Never use the extreme reverse force (‑1.0).**  
   The ‑1.0 action costs a penalty of 0.1 × 1² = 0.1 per step, which quickly erodes the final +100 reward. The ‑0.8 force (penalty = 0.064) is enough to pull the car back when it is sliding downhill.

2. **Taper the forward force as the car gets close to the goal.**  
   When the car is already past p ≈ 0.35 the momentum needed to cross the 0.45 threshold is small. Continuing to push with 0.6 adds unnecessary penalty; stepping down to 0.5 and finally to 0.3 lets the car coast over the hill while keeping the penalty low.

3. **Apply a modest forward thrust as soon as the velocity is positive.**  
   As soon as the car has any forward velocity (v > 0) the policy switches to a constant 0.6 thrust until the position reaches the “taper zone”. This quickly builds the kinetic energy needed to climb the hill without overshooting.

**What did not work?**

* The “‑1.0” reverse thrust in Policy 2 never gave a measurable advantage and only increased the cumulative penalty.  
* The “else a = 0.0” branch in Policy 1 sometimes leaves the car idle in the middle of the valley (when v ≈ 0 but p > ‑0.20). In practice the car should always receive a small corrective push (either forward or backward) to keep the oscillation going.

**Conclusion**

Policy 3 is the clear winner.  It already embodies the two most important ideas (no ‑1.0, taper forward thrust).  A small refinement—adding a tiny “coast” zone just before the goal and a modest reverse thrust when the car is stuck on the left side—can shave off a few extra hundredths of penalty and push the average reward even closer to the theoretical maximum (≈ 99.9).

---

## Updated rule‑based strategy (the “Refined Policy”)

```
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
#  MountainCar – continuous force ∈ [‑1.0 , 1.0]
#  Goal:  p >= 0.45   (receive +100 reward)
#  Penalty per step:  -0.1 * a^2
# -----------------------------------------------------------------

IF   p >= 0.45                         THEN a = 0.0                # already at goal → stop
ELIF p >= 0.40  AND v > 0.0            THEN a = 0.30               # final “coast” zone
ELIF p >= 0.35  AND v > 0.0            THEN a = 0.45               # gentle taper
ELIF p >= 0.30  AND v > 0.0            THEN a = 0.55               # start tapering
ELIF v > 0.0                           THEN a = 0.60               # full forward thrust while moving right
ELIF v <= 0.0  AND p > -0.20           THEN a = -0.75               # push left when sliding back on the right side
ELIF v <= 0.0  AND p <= -0.20          THEN a = -0.70               # weaker reverse thrust deep in the left valley
ELIF v > 0.0   AND p <= -0.20          THEN a = 0.55                # small forward thrust to climb out of the left dip
ELSE                                   a = 0.0                    # safety fallback (should never be hit)
```

### Why these numbers?

| Condition | Action | Reason |
|-----------|--------|--------|
| **p ≥ 0.45** | 0.0 | Goal reached – any extra force only adds penalty. |
| **0.40 ≤ p < 0.45, v > 0** | 0.30 | The car already has enough kinetic energy; a small push keeps it moving over the summit without extra cost. |
| **0.35 ≤ p < 0.40, v > 0** | 0.45 | Slightly stronger than the final zone, still a reduction from the “full‑thrust” 0.6. |
| **0.30 ≤ p < 0.35, v > 0** | 0.55 | Begin the taper; 0.55 is enough to keep acceleration but saves ≈ 0.01 per step compared with 0.60. |
| **v > 0, p < 0.30** | 0.60 | Full forward thrust while the car is still gaining speed in the valley. |
| **v ≤ 0, p > ‑0.20** | –0.75 | When the car is sliding back on the right side we need a strong enough pull to reverse direction, but we avoid the harsher –1.0 to keep the penalty low. |
| **v ≤ 0, p ≤ ‑0.20** | –0.70 | Deep in the left valley the car tends to “stick”. A slightly weaker reverse force is sufficient and costs less. |
| **v > 0, p ≤ ‑0.20** | 0.55 | The car has just managed to move rightwards out of the left dip; a modest thrust prevents overshooting back to the left. |
| **else** | 0.0 | Catch‑all; should never be used in practice. |

### Expected effect

* **Accelerate quickly** from the bottom of the valley (0.6) → build the momentum needed to climb the right hill.
* **Reduce thrust gradually** as the car approaches the summit → lower the per‑step penalty while still guaranteeing enough speed to cross the 0.45 threshold.
* **Avoid the extreme –1.0** reverse force → keep the cumulative penalty well below the 7‑point loss observed in the earlier policies.
* **Provide a small “coast” push** (0.30) in the final 0.05 m of the hill → the car will glide over the top with almost no extra cost, maximizing the net reward.

---

