| **Mid‑valley, still moving left** (v < 0) | `‑k_vel·v` becomes **positive**, raw stays > 0 → a ≈ +0.55‑+0.60 | +0.55‑+0.60 | Keeps the car accelerating rightward while still damping the left‑ward speed. |
| **Climbing the right hill, moderate speed** (p ≈ 0.2‑0.35, v ≈ 0.04‑0.06) | Position error ≈ 0.1‑0.25 → raw ≈ 0.12‑0.30 → a ≈ 0.12‑0.30 | 0.12‑0.30 | The thrust is now *much* smaller than the old “+0.55” constant, which reduces the ‑0.1·a² penalty while still giving enough push to reach the summit. |
| **Very close to the goal** (p ≈ 0.42‑0.44, v ≈ 0.04) | Position error ≈ 0.01‑0.03 → raw ≈ 0.01‑0.02 → a ≈ 0.01‑0.02 | ≈ 0.01‑0.02 | Near‑zero force – the car coasts the last few centimeters, avoiding overshoot and paying almost no action penalty. |
| **If we ever start sliding back** (v < 0 while p < 0.45) | `‑k_vel·v` becomes **negative**, raw may become negative → a ≈ ‑0.4‑‑0.6 (clipped) | –0.4 to ‑0.6 | The policy automatically applies a modest reverse thrust to brake the backward slide, similar to the “‑0.45 / ‑0.65” branches of the old rules. |

**Why this should beat the previous best (≈ 95.92):**  

